Q: Change code to filter via multiple attributes A: <p>There are many options with this one.</p>
<p>The first one that comes to mind is using the <code>.filter()</code> function.</p>
<p>Update the jQuery selector to select the <code>span</code> element or add a third <code>data-*</code> attribute or special class to select all of the items you want to search.</p>
<p>Then once you have your list of items, you can easily run them through the <code>filter()</code> function like the example below. The below code snippets adds the <code>hidden</code> class to all items and then removes it specifically for the items that match your search value.</p>
<p>Another approach, which would be a bigger change but easier to maintain is to have two different lists. The first list contains everything and the second list is what you render. You then filter the first list and assign the filtered list to the list that is being rendered. With this approach, you won't have any add class/remove class logic which is a bit cleaner.</p>
<pre><code>if (searchVal) {
    filterItems.addClass('hidden');

    filterItems.filter(function () {
      const title = ($(this).attr('title') || '').toLowerCase();
      const emoji = ($(this).data('emoji') || '').toString().toLowerCase();
      const version = ($(this).attr('version') || '').toLowerCase();

      return (
        title.includes(searchVal) ||
        emoji.includes(searchVal) ||
        version.includes(searchVal)
      );
    }).removeClass('hidden');

} else {
    filterItems.removeClass('hidden');
}
</code></pre>
